addSubject({id:'his', topics:[{"t": "Exam skills: sources, cartoons and essays", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>answer source questions (identify, explain, evaluate usefulness and reliability)</li><li>interpret cartoons and photographs</li><li>write a structured historical essay (300–400 words)</li></ul></div><h3>1. Paper structure</h3><p>Question 1 (the Cold War, 1945–1990) is <b>compulsory</b> (50 marks). Then answer <b>two</b> of Questions 2–6 (35 marks each). Each question: Part A and B source questions, Part C (or D) essay.</p><h3>2. Source questions</h3><table><tr><th>Question type</th><th>How to answer</th></tr><tr><td>Identify / name</td><td>short, exact answer from the source</td></tr><tr><td>Explain a phrase</td><td>put it in your own words + context</td></tr><tr><td>What does the cartoonist think?</td><td>describe the symbols → state the message → link to the historical event</td></tr><tr><td>Usefulness / reliability</td><td>who made it, when, why (purpose), bias, what it shows and what it leaves out</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Cartoon of Stalin blocked by \"Truman Doctrine\": the cartoonist shows the USA stopping Soviet expansion → message: containment of communism (1947).</div><h3>3. Essay writing</h3><table><tr><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr><tr><td>Introduction</td><td>background + answer the question in one sentence (thesis) + outline your 3 points</td></tr><tr><td>Body paragraphs (3)</td><td><b>PEEL</b>: Point – Evidence (dates, names, events, statistics) – Explanation – Link back to the question</td></tr><tr><td>Conclusion</td><td>sum up and give your judgement (which factor was most important and why)</td></tr></table><div class=\"tip\">💡 Use specific evidence: \"Berlin Blockade (June 1948 – May 1949)\" earns more than \"a crisis in Berlin\".</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "How many questions must you answer?", "o": ["2", "3", "5", "6"], "a": 1, "e": "Q1 + any two from Q2–6."}, {"q": "Short essay length", "o": ["100 words", "300–500 words", "1000 words", "50 words"], "a": 1, "e": "About 1 page."}, {"q": "\"To what extent\" asks for…", "o": ["a list", "a balanced judgement", "a definition", "a date"], "a": 1, "e": "Weigh the factor against others."}, {"q": "The first step with a cartoon is to…", "o": ["write an essay", "identify the figures and symbols", "count the words", "skip it"], "a": 1, "e": "Then the message, then the context."}]}, {"t": "The Cold War, 1945–1990", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain the ideologies of the USA (capitalism, democracy) and USSR (communism)</li><li>describe the origins of the Cold War and containment (Truman Doctrine, Marshall Plan)</li><li>explain the crises: Berlin Blockade, Korean War, Berlin Wall, Cuban Missile Crisis, Vietnam</li><li>explain the arms race, détente and the end of the Cold War</li></ul></div><h3>1. Two ideologies</h3><table><tr><th>USA</th><th>USSR</th></tr><tr><td>capitalism: private ownership, free market, profit</td><td>communism: state ownership, planned economy, no private profit</td></tr><tr><td>democracy: free elections, many parties, free speech</td><td>one-party state, censorship, secret police</td></tr><tr><td>aim: contain communism, open markets</td><td>aim: security, spread communism, buffer zone</td></tr></table><h3>2. Timeline</h3><table class=\"tl\"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr><tr><td><b>1945</b></td><td>Yalta and Potsdam conferences; atomic bombs on Japan; Europe divided</td></tr><tr><td><b>1946</b></td><td>Churchill's \"Iron Curtain\" speech</td></tr><tr><td><b>1947</b></td><td>Truman Doctrine (aid to Greece and Turkey); Marshall Plan ($13 billion for Europe)</td></tr><tr><td><b>1948–49</b></td><td>Berlin Blockade → Berlin Airlift</td></tr><tr><td><b>1949</b></td><td>NATO formed; USSR tests atomic bomb; China becomes communist</td></tr><tr><td><b>1950–53</b></td><td>Korean War</td></tr><tr><td><b>1955</b></td><td>Warsaw Pact</td></tr><tr><td><b>1956</b></td><td>Hungarian uprising crushed</td></tr><tr><td><b>1957</b></td><td>Sputnik – space race</td></tr><tr><td><b>1961</b></td><td>Berlin Wall built</td></tr><tr><td><b>1962</b></td><td>Cuban Missile Crisis</td></tr><tr><td><b>1963</b></td><td>Hotline; Partial Test Ban Treaty</td></tr><tr><td><b>1965–73</b></td><td>US war in Vietnam</td></tr><tr><td><b>1970s</b></td><td>Détente: SALT I (1972), Helsinki (1975)</td></tr><tr><td><b>1979</b></td><td>USSR invades Afghanistan</td></tr><tr><td><b>1985</b></td><td>Gorbachev: glasnost and perestroika</td></tr><tr><td><b>1989</b></td><td>Berlin Wall falls</td></tr><tr><td><b>1991</b></td><td>USSR collapses</td></tr></table><h3>3. Key crises explained</h3><table><tr><th>Crisis</th><th>Cause</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td>Berlin Blockade 1948–49</td><td>Stalin cut road and rail links to West Berlin after the new currency</td><td>US/UK airlift 2.3 m tonnes; Stalin backed down; NATO formed</td></tr><tr><td>Korean War 1950–53</td><td>North (USSR/China) invaded South (USA/UN)</td><td>stalemate at the 38th parallel; ~3 m dead; global containment</td></tr><tr><td>Berlin Wall 1961</td><td>2.5–3 m East Germans fled West through Berlin</td><td>wall stopped the exodus; symbol of the Iron Curtain until 1989</td></tr><tr><td>Cuban Missile Crisis 1962</td><td>Soviet missiles in Cuba, 145 km from the USA</td><td>13-day stand-off; missiles removed, US removed missiles from Turkey; hotline</td></tr></table><h3>4. The arms race and the end</h3><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Pacific connection</b>Nuclear weapons made both sides afraid of war → Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD) → proxy wars instead of direct war. Pacific link: nuclear tests at Kiritimati (UK/US 1957–62), Bikini (USA), Moruroa (France).</div><p>The end: Soviet economy weak (arms spending, Afghanistan), Gorbachev's reforms, people's revolutions in Eastern Europe in 1989, reunification of Germany 1990, USSR dissolved Dec 1991.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Communist China was proclaimed in…", "o": ["1945", "1949", "1956", "1962"], "a": 1, "e": "1 October 1949, Mao Zedong."}, {"q": "The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan began in…", "o": ["1968", "1979", "1985", "1989"], "a": 1, "e": "December 1979."}, {"q": "Which event ended détente?", "o": ["SALT I", "Soviet invasion of Afghanistan", "Helsinki Accords", "Nixon in China"], "a": 1, "e": "US boycott of the 1980 Olympics, SALT II shelved."}, {"q": "The Berlin Wall fell on…", "o": ["9 November 1989", "3 October 1990", "25 December 1991", "13 August 1961"], "a": 0, "e": "The wall was built in 1961."}, {"q": "Gorbachev's policy of openness was…", "o": ["perestroika", "glasnost", "détente", "containment"], "a": 1, "e": "Perestroika = restructuring."}, {"q": "The 1987 treaty that removed intermediate-range nuclear missiles", "o": ["SALT I", "INF Treaty", "Test Ban Treaty", "START II"], "a": 1, "e": "Reagan and Gorbachev."}]}, {"t": "Causes of World War I, 1900–1914", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain the long-term causes of WWI: militarism, alliances, imperialism, nationalism (MAIN)</li><li>explain the short-term cause: the assassination at Sarajevo and the July crisis</li><li>explain how the alliance system and Schlieffen Plan turned a local war into a world war</li></ul></div><h3>1. MAIN causes</h3><table><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Evidence</th></tr><tr><td>Militarism</td><td>naval race: Britain's Dreadnought (1906) vs German fleet; huge conscript armies; war plans</td></tr><tr><td>Alliances</td><td>Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy – 1882) vs Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain – 1907)</td></tr><tr><td>Imperialism</td><td>competition for colonies: Moroccan crises 1905 and 1911</td></tr><tr><td>Nationalism</td><td>Slav nationalism in the Balkans (Serbia, Black Hand); French revenge for Alsace-Lorraine; German pride</td></tr></table><h3>2. Timeline</h3><table class=\"tl\"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr><tr><td><b>1871</b></td><td>Germany unified; France loses Alsace-Lorraine</td></tr><tr><td><b>1879/1882</b></td><td>Dual Alliance → Triple Alliance (Bismarck isolates France)</td></tr><tr><td><b>1904</b></td><td>Entente Cordiale (Britain–France)</td></tr><tr><td><b>1905</b></td><td>First Moroccan crisis</td></tr><tr><td><b>1906</b></td><td>HMS Dreadnought launched</td></tr><tr><td><b>1907</b></td><td>Triple Entente</td></tr><tr><td><b>1908</b></td><td>Austria annexes Bosnia</td></tr><tr><td><b>1911</b></td><td>Agadir (second Moroccan) crisis</td></tr><tr><td><b>1912–13</b></td><td>Balkan Wars</td></tr><tr><td><b>28 Jun 1914</b></td><td>Archduke Franz Ferdinand assassinated in Sarajevo by Gavrilo Princip</td></tr><tr><td><b>28 Jul 1914</b></td><td>Austria declares war on Serbia</td></tr><tr><td><b>1–3 Aug 1914</b></td><td>Germany declares war on Russia and France; invades Belgium</td></tr><tr><td><b>4 Aug 1914</b></td><td>Britain declares war (Treaty of London 1839 – \"scrap of paper\")</td></tr></table><h3>3. Chain reaction</h3><p>Austria (backed by Germany's \"blank cheque\") attacked Serbia → Russia mobilised to protect Serbia → Germany declared war on Russia and France → the <b>Schlieffen Plan</b> sent German troops through neutral Belgium to defeat France quickly → Britain entered to defend Belgium. Within a week a Balkan quarrel became a European war.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "The Triple Entente was Britain, France and…", "o": ["Germany", "Russia", "Italy", "Austria"], "a": 1, "e": "Completed in 1907."}, {"q": "The First Moroccan Crisis began in…", "o": ["1898", "1905", "1911", "1914"], "a": 1, "e": "The Kaiser at Tangier."}, {"q": "Britain entered WWI after Germany invaded…", "o": ["France", "Belgium", "Serbia", "Russia"], "a": 1, "e": "Treaty of London 1839."}, {"q": "Franz Ferdinand was assassinated in…", "o": ["Vienna", "Berlin", "Sarajevo", "Belgrade"], "a": 2, "e": "28 June 1914."}, {"q": "The German plan to defeat France quickly through Belgium", "o": ["Schlieffen Plan", "Plan XVII", "Marshall Plan", "Dawes Plan"], "a": 0, "e": "Avoiding a two-front war."}]}, {"t": "Origins of World War II, 1919–1941", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain the Treaty of Versailles and German reactions</li><li>evaluate the League of Nations and collective security in the 1920s</li><li>explain the rise of dictators and the failures of the 1930s</li><li>explain appeasement and the outbreak of war in 1939–41</li></ul></div><h3>1. Treaty of Versailles (1919)</h3><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>War guilt (Art. 231)</td><td>Germany blamed for the war</td></tr><tr><td>Reparations</td><td>£6.6 billion (1921)</td></tr><tr><td>Army</td><td>limited to 100 000; no air force, tanks or submarines</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td>lost Alsace-Lorraine, Polish Corridor, all colonies; Rhineland demilitarised</td></tr></table><p>Germans called it a \"<b>Diktat</b>\" (dictated peace). Resentment helped Hitler rise.</p><h3>2. Collective security in the 1920s</h3><table><tr><th>Attempt</th><th>Weakness</th></tr><tr><td>League of Nations (1920)</td><td>USA never joined; no army; needed unanimous decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Washington Naval Conference (1921–22)</td><td>no enforcement; Japan unhappy</td></tr><tr><td>Locarno Treaties (1925)</td><td>only western borders guaranteed</td></tr><tr><td>Kellogg–Briand Pact (1928)</td><td>no punishment for breaking it</td></tr></table><h3>3. Road to war – timeline</h3><table class=\"tl\"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr><tr><td><b>1929</b></td><td>Wall Street Crash → Great Depression</td></tr><tr><td><b>1931</b></td><td>Japan invades Manchuria</td></tr><tr><td><b>1933</b></td><td>Hitler becomes Chancellor; Germany leaves the League</td></tr><tr><td><b>1935</b></td><td>Italy invades Abyssinia; Germany rearms</td></tr><tr><td><b>1936</b></td><td>Rhineland remilitarised</td></tr><tr><td><b>1938</b></td><td>Anschluss with Austria; Munich Agreement gives Sudetenland</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mar 1939</b></td><td>Germany takes the rest of Czechoslovakia</td></tr><tr><td><b>23 Aug 1939</b></td><td>Nazi–Soviet Pact</td></tr><tr><td><b>1 Sep 1939</b></td><td>Germany invades Poland → Britain & France declare war</td></tr><tr><td><b>22 Jun 1941</b></td><td>Operation Barbarossa</td></tr><tr><td><b>7 Dec 1941</b></td><td>Pearl Harbor → USA enters</td></tr></table><p><b>Appeasement</b>: Britain and France gave in to Hitler's demands to avoid war (memories of WWI, unprepared military, sympathy with German complaints). It failed: Hitler took it as weakness.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Italy invaded Abyssinia in…", "o": ["1931", "1935", "1938", "1939"], "a": 1, "e": "Mussolini."}, {"q": "The Munich Agreement gave Hitler…", "o": ["Poland", "the Sudetenland", "Austria", "the Rhineland"], "a": 1, "e": "September 1938."}, {"q": "The Nazi-Soviet Pact was signed in…", "o": ["August 1939", "September 1938", "June 1941", "March 1939"], "a": 0, "e": "23 August 1939."}, {"q": "The pact ended when Germany launched…", "o": ["Blitzkrieg on France", "Operation Barbarossa", "Sea Lion", "Pearl Harbor"], "a": 1, "e": "22 June 1941."}, {"q": "Appeasement means…", "o": ["standing up to aggressors", "giving in to demands to avoid war", "forming alliances", "collective security"], "a": 1, "e": "Chamberlain's policy."}]}, {"t": "Gandhi and modern India, 1915–1947", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe British rule in India and the growth of nationalism (INC, Muslim League)</li><li>explain Gandhi's methods: satyagraha, non-cooperation, civil disobedience, Quit India</li><li>explain the causes and results of Partition, 1947</li></ul></div><h3>1. Background</h3><p>Britain ruled India (the Raj). The <b>Indian National Congress</b> (1885) first asked for more rights, later independence. The <b>Amritsar Massacre</b> (1919: ~379 killed by British troops) turned many against British rule.</p><h3>2. Gandhi's methods</h3><table><tr><th>Campaign</th><th>Date</th><th>What happened</th></tr><tr><td>Satyagraha (truth-force)</td><td>from 1915</td><td>non-violent resistance</td></tr><tr><td>Non-Cooperation</td><td>1920–22</td><td>boycott British goods, schools, courts; spin khadi; ended after Chauri Chaura violence</td></tr><tr><td>Salt March (civil disobedience)</td><td>1930</td><td>388 km to Dandi to make salt; 60 000 arrested; world attention</td></tr><tr><td>Round Table Conference</td><td>1931</td><td>Gandhi in London; little gained</td></tr><tr><td>Quit India</td><td>1942</td><td>demand Britain leave now; leaders jailed</td></tr></table><p>Other tactics: fasting, hartals (strikes), boycotts.</p><h3>3. Independence and Partition</h3><p>After WWII Britain was weak and could not hold India. Muslim League leader <b>Muhammad Ali Jinnah</b> demanded a separate Muslim state. Direct Action Day (Aug 1946) brought riots. On <b>15 August 1947</b> India and Pakistan became independent. 10–15 million people moved; up to 1 million died in communal violence. Gandhi fasted for peace and was assassinated on 30 January 1948.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Gandhi's method of non-violent resistance", "o": ["swaraj", "satyagraha", "hartal", "khadi"], "a": 1, "e": "Truth-force."}, {"q": "The Salt March took place in…", "o": ["1919", "1930", "1942", "1947"], "a": 1, "e": "Civil disobedience."}, {"q": "The 1942 campaign demanding that the British leave immediately", "o": ["Non-cooperation", "Quit India", "Khilafat", "Salt March"], "a": 1, "e": "Congress leaders were arrested."}, {"q": "The leader of the Muslim League who demanded Pakistan", "o": ["Nehru", "Jinnah", "Patel", "Bose"], "a": 1, "e": "Muhammad Ali Jinnah."}, {"q": "India gained independence and was partitioned in…", "o": ["1945", "1946", "1947", "1950"], "a": 2, "e": "15 August 1947."}]}, {"t": "The USA, 1920–1945", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe the economic boom of the 1920s and its causes</li><li>explain the Wall Street Crash and the Great Depression</li><li>describe Roosevelt's New Deal</li><li>explain US foreign policy from isolation to war, 1939–45</li></ul></div><h3>1. The Roaring Twenties</h3><p>Causes of the boom: mass production (Ford assembly line), new industries (cars, radios, electrical goods), hire purchase/credit, advertising, laissez-faire policies, low taxes, high tariffs (Fordney–McCumber 1922). But: farmers, Black Americans and many workers did not share the wealth.</p><h3>2. Crash and Depression</h3><p><b>Wall Street Crash, October 1929</b>: share prices collapsed after speculation on borrowed money. Banks failed, businesses closed; by 1933 about 13 million (25%) were unemployed. Causes: overproduction, unequal wealth, too much credit, speculation.</p><h3>3. The New Deal (1933–)</h3><p>Roosevelt's \"3 Rs\": Relief, Recovery, Reform – e.g. CCC (jobs for young men), TVA (dams and electricity), Social Security Act (1935), banking reforms.</p><h3>4. From isolation to war</h3><table class=\"tl\"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr><tr><td><b>1935–37</b></td><td>Neutrality Acts</td></tr><tr><td><b>Nov 1939</b></td><td>Cash and Carry</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sep 1940</b></td><td>Destroyers-for-Bases deal</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mar 1941</b></td><td>Lend-Lease: \"arsenal of democracy\"</td></tr><tr><td><b>Aug 1941</b></td><td>Atlantic Charter</td></tr><tr><td><b>7 Dec 1941</b></td><td>Pearl Harbor → USA declares war</td></tr><tr><td><b>1942–45</b></td><td>Pacific campaign: Midway, Guadalcanal, Tarawa (Nov 1943), Iwo Jima, Okinawa</td></tr><tr><td><b>Aug 1945</b></td><td>atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Producing large quantities on an assembly line is…", "o": ["mass production", "laissez-faire", "hire purchase", "speculation"], "a": 0, "e": "Ford's Model T."}, {"q": "The Wall Street Crash happened in…", "o": ["1921", "1929", "1933", "1941"], "a": 1, "e": "October 1929."}, {"q": "Lending weapons to Britain from 1941 was…", "o": ["Cash and Carry", "Lend-Lease", "the New Deal", "the Neutrality Acts"], "a": 1, "e": "March 1941."}, {"q": "The USA entered WWII after…", "o": ["the invasion of Poland", "Pearl Harbor", "the fall of France", "D-Day"], "a": 1, "e": "7 December 1941."}, {"q": "Government not interfering in the economy", "o": ["the New Deal", "laissez-faire", "tariffs", "isolationism"], "a": 1, "e": "The 1920s Republicans."}]}, {"t": "Japan, 1912–1945", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe Japan's political and economic development, 1912–1930s</li><li>explain the rise of militarism and expansion in China</li><li>explain the Pacific War and Japan's defeat</li><li>describe the US occupation and its effects</li></ul></div><h3>1. Japan to the 1930s</h3><p>Taishō democracy (1912–26): political parties, universal male suffrage (1925). WWI brought an economic boom and German colonies (Shandong, Pacific islands). The Depression hit silk exports; the army gained power, blaming weak politicians.</p><h3>2. Expansion – timeline</h3><table class=\"tl\"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr><tr><td><b>1915</b></td><td>Twenty-One Demands on China</td></tr><tr><td><b>1931</b></td><td>Manchurian Incident → Manchukuo</td></tr><tr><td><b>1933</b></td><td>Japan leaves the League of Nations</td></tr><tr><td><b>1937</b></td><td>full war with China (Nanjing massacre)</td></tr><tr><td><b>1940</b></td><td>Tripartite Pact with Germany and Italy</td></tr><tr><td><b>1941</b></td><td>US oil embargo; 7 Dec Pearl Harbor</td></tr><tr><td><b>1941–42</b></td><td>takes Malaya, Singapore, Philippines, Dutch East Indies, Gilbert Islands (Kiribati)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Jun 1942</b></td><td>defeat at Midway</td></tr><tr><td><b>Nov 1943</b></td><td>Battle of Tarawa (Betio): ~6000 killed in 76 hours</td></tr><tr><td><b>Aug 1945</b></td><td>atomic bombs; USSR enters; Emperor's broadcast 15 Aug; surrender 2 Sep</td></tr></table><h3>3. Defeat and occupation</h3><p>Reasons for defeat: US industrial strength, Midway, island-hopping, submarine war on shipping, bombing of cities, atomic bombs. <b>US occupation (1945–52)</b> under MacArthur: disarmament, war-crimes trials, new constitution (1947) with Article 9 renouncing war, land reform, the Emperor as a symbol only.</p><div class=\"tip\">💡 Kiribati link: the Battle of Tarawa (20–23 November 1943) at Betio – you can name it as evidence of the island-hopping campaign.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "All adult men in Japan got the vote in…", "o": ["1912", "1919", "1925", "1945"], "a": 2, "e": "The General Election Law."}, {"q": "Japan invaded Manchuria in…", "o": ["1915", "1931", "1937", "1941"], "a": 1, "e": "The Mukden Incident."}, {"q": "The turning-point naval battle in the Pacific (1942)", "o": ["Coral Sea", "Midway", "Leyte", "Tarawa"], "a": 1, "e": "Four Japanese carriers were sunk."}, {"q": "The Battle of Tarawa was in…", "o": ["1941", "1942", "1943", "1945"], "a": 2, "e": "November 1943, Betio."}, {"q": "Japan surrendered after atomic bombs were dropped on…", "o": ["Tokyo and Osaka", "Hiroshima and Nagasaki", "Kyoto and Kobe", "Okinawa"], "a": 1, "e": "August 1945."}]}],
papers:[
{y:2025, src:'P2025/KSSC History 2025.pdf', note:'Resources (cartoons, photos and texts) are in the original paper. Essays are given as model plans.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – The search for security in the nuclear age (compulsory, 50 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Central figure planting the flag (Birth of Communist China).',a:'<b>Mao Zedong</b>'},
{n:'A2.',q:'One superpower\'s reaction to the planting of the flag.',a:'The USA was alarmed: it saw the "loss of China" as a huge communist gain. It refused to recognise the People\'s Republic (it backed Chiang Kai-shek in Taiwan), increased containment in Asia, and anti-communist fear at home (McCarthyism) grew. (The USSR welcomed it and signed the Sino-Soviet Treaty in 1950.)'},
{n:'A3.',q:'Significance of the cartoon for understanding Cold War tensions.',a:'It shows communism spreading into Asia: the most populous country turned communist. This alarmed the West, widened the Cold War from Europe to Asia and increased the fear of a "domino effect".'},
{n:'A4.',q:'One country the startled figures represent.',a:'The <b>USA</b> (or Britain / the Western powers).'},
{n:'A5.',q:'How did the birth of communist China change superpower relations?',a:'It strengthened the communist bloc (Sino-Soviet Treaty, 1950) and made the USA adopt a stronger containment policy in Asia: NSC-68, involvement in the Korean War (1950–53), support for Taiwan and later SEATO (1954). Tensions and the arms race grew, and the Cold War became global.'},
{n:'B1.',q:'Superpowers\' reactions to the Soviet-Afghan War.',a:'USSR: invaded in December 1979 to prop up the communist government. USA: condemned it (the Carter Doctrine), boycotted the 1980 Moscow Olympics, imposed a grain embargo, did not ratify SALT II, and secretly armed the Afghan Mujahideen (with Stinger missiles under Reagan).'},
{n:'B2.',q:'One impact on the Afghans mentioned in the text.',a:'About a million Afghans lost their lives (and millions fled abroad as refugees).'},
{n:'B3.',q:'Explain "major humiliation of the Red Army\'s military power".',a:'A superpower army failed to defeat poorly equipped guerrilla fighters after nearly 10 years, and had to withdraw (1989) having lost about 15,000 soldiers. This damaged Soviet prestige; it became the USSR\'s "Vietnam".'},
{n:'B4.',q:'One reason for Soviet interest in Afghanistan.',a:'To keep a friendly communist government on its southern border and stop Islamic fundamentalism (after Iran\'s 1979 revolution) spreading into Soviet Central Asia. (Also: access towards the Indian Ocean and the oil of the Gulf region.)'},
{n:'B5.',q:'Effect of the war on détente.',a:'It ended détente. The USA saw the invasion as aggression, so SALT II was abandoned, the Olympics were boycotted and arms spending increased. A "Second Cold War" began under Reagan in the early 1980s.'},
{n:'C1.',q:'Year the Berlin Wall fell.',a:'<b>1989</b> (9 November 1989)'},
{n:'C2.',q:'Importance of drawing Reagan in the hole of the Wall.',a:'It suggests Reagan personally broke through the Wall, giving him the credit for ending the Cold War (recalling his 1987 Berlin speech: "Mr Gorbachev, tear down this wall!").'},
{n:'C3.',q:'Importance of the fall of the Wall in ending the Cold War.',a:'The Wall was the biggest symbol of the divided Europe and the Iron Curtain. Its fall showed that the USSR would no longer use force to control Eastern Europe. Communist governments collapsed, Germany reunited in 1990, and the Warsaw Pact ended, marking the end of the Cold War.'},
{n:'C4–5.',q:'Change in superpower relations after 1989; one way US-Soviet relations improved.',a:'Relations changed from rivalry to cooperation. At the Malta Summit (December 1989) Bush and Gorbachev declared the Cold War over. They signed START I (1991) to cut strategic nuclear weapons, and cooperated over German reunification and the Gulf War (1990–91).'},
{n:'C6.',q:'Impact of the destruction of the Wall on the superpowers.',a:'USSR: it lost control of Eastern Europe and its prestige, nationalist movements grew inside the USSR, and it collapsed in December 1991. USA: it became the only superpower, NATO expanded, and its influence grew worldwide.'},
{n:'C7.',q:'How does the cartoon exaggerate Reagan\'s role?',a:'It shows Reagan alone breaking the Wall. In reality it was opened by East German crowds and officials. Gorbachev\'s reforms (glasnost, perestroika, refusing to send troops), the people\'s protests (Leipzig) and the USSR\'s economic problems were at least as important as Reagan\'s pressure.'},
{n:'D.',q:'Essay: assess superpower relations 1970s–1990s; to what extent did they contribute to the collapse of the USSR?',a:'<b>Intro:</b> relations moved from détente (1970s) to renewed tension (1979–85) and then cooperation (1985–91). They helped bring about the Soviet collapse, but internal weaknesses mattered more.\n<b>Part 1, state of relations:</b>\n• Détente: SALT I (1972), the Helsinki Accords (1975), trade and grain deals.\n• Second Cold War: Afghanistan (1979), the Olympic boycotts, Reagan\'s "evil empire", the military build-up, SDI.\n• Thaw: Gorbachev (1985); summits (Geneva, Reykjavik), the INF Treaty (1987), withdrawal from Afghanistan (1989), Malta (1989).\n<b>Part 2, contribution to the collapse:</b>\n• The arms race and SDI forced huge military spending (about 15–25% of GDP) that the weak Soviet economy could not afford.\n• The Afghan war drained money and lives and damaged morale.\n• Better relations let Gorbachev cut forces and allow Eastern Europe its freedom (the Sinatra Doctrine), which encouraged nationalism inside the USSR.\n<b>Other factors:</b> economic stagnation, Chernobyl (1986), glasnost exposing problems, nationalism in the republics, the failed August 1991 coup.\n<b>Conclusion:</b> superpower relations contributed to a large extent through economic pressure, but the USSR mainly collapsed because of its own economic and political weaknesses (December 1991).'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Causes of World War I (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1–3.',q:'The small boy; the event; the treaty violated.',a:'1. <b>Belgium</b>\n2. The German invasion of Belgium (August 1914, the Schlieffen Plan)\n3. The <b>Treaty of London (1839)</b>, which guaranteed Belgian neutrality'},
{n:'A4.',q:'How did this incident contribute to WWI?',a:'Germany invaded neutral Belgium to reach France quickly. Britain had promised to protect Belgian neutrality, so it declared war on Germany on 4 August 1914. This turned a continental war into a world war, bringing in the British Empire.'},
{n:'A5.',q:'Meaning of "No Thoroughfare" on the gate.',a:'Belgium refused to let German troops pass through its territory: it defended its neutrality even though it was small.'},
{n:'A6.',q:'The political purpose of the cartoon in Britain in 1914.',a:'Propaganda: it showed Germany as a bully and Belgium as a brave victim, to justify Britain\'s entry into the war on moral grounds ("gallant little Belgium") and to encourage public support and volunteers to enlist.'},
{n:'B1.',q:'Year the First Moroccan Crisis began.',a:'<b>1905</b> (the Kaiser\'s visit to Tangier)'},
{n:'B2.',q:'A phrase suggesting Germany feared exclusion.',a:'"…she will retire empty-handed from the partition of the world" (or "Is the German man-in-the-street to get nothing?").'},
{n:'B3–4.',q:'Why Germany wanted Morocco; meaning of "peg out claims".',a:'3. Germany wanted colonies and naval bases for its growing population, power and trade, so it would not be left out of the scramble for colonies.\n4. "Peg out claims" means marking out territory for itself: Germany intended to grab colonies and challenge France\'s influence in Morocco.'},
{n:'B5–6.',q:'Effects of the crisis on the Great Powers; on Germany\'s relations with Britain and France.',a:'At the Algeciras Conference (1906) Germany was isolated: only Austria supported it, and France kept control of Morocco. Instead of breaking the Entente Cordiale, the crisis made Britain and France closer, with secret military talks. Germany\'s relations with both worsened, fears of encirclement grew, and the 1907 Anglo-Russian Entente followed.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: diplomatic developments from the late 1870s to the early 1900s; how they turned a Balkan crisis into a European war.',a:'<b>Part 1:</b>\n• Dual Alliance 1879 (Germany–Austria) → Triple Alliance 1882 (+ Italy).\n• Franco-Russian Alliance 1894 (after Bismarck\'s Reinsurance Treaty lapsed in 1890).\n• Entente Cordiale 1904 and Anglo-Russian Entente 1907 → Triple Entente. The Moroccan Crises (1905, 1911) tightened these blocs.\n<b>Part 2:</b>\n• Sarajevo, 28 June 1914 → Germany gave Austria a "blank cheque" → ultimatum to Serbia.\n• Russia mobilised to protect Serbia (Pan-Slavism) → Germany declared war on Russia (1 August) and France (3 August).\n• The Schlieffen Plan → invasion of Belgium → Britain declared war (4 August).\n• The alliances turned a local Balkan quarrel into a chain reaction involving all the Great Powers; mobilisation timetables and militarism left little time for diplomacy.\n<b>Conclusion:</b> the alliance system did not cause the crisis alone, but it made a general war very likely.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Origins of World War II (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1–2.',q:'The main figure; the event.',a:'1. <b>Benito Mussolini</b>\n2. The <b>Abyssinian Crisis</b>: Italy\'s invasion of Abyssinia (Ethiopia), 1935'},
{n:'A3–4.',q:'How the cartoon criticises the League; the League\'s response.',a:'3. It shows the League and the world standing by and watching while Abyssinia "burns": it is powerless and does nothing effective.\n4. The League condemned Italy and imposed economic sanctions, but they were weak: oil was not included, the Suez Canal stayed open, and Britain and France secretly proposed the Hoare-Laval Pact giving Italy much of Abyssinia.'},
{n:'A5–6.',q:'How the crisis contributed to WWII; how it encouraged dictators.',a:'5. It destroyed the League\'s credibility as a peace-keeper and pushed Italy towards Germany (Rome-Berlin Axis 1936). Collective security collapsed.\n6. Hitler saw that aggression went unpunished: he remilitarised the Rhineland (March 1936) while the world was distracted, and Japan and Germany became bolder.'},
{n:'B1–3.',q:'Event giving Hitler confidence; year of the Pact; the two invaders of Poland.',a:'1. The signing of the <b>Nazi-Soviet Pact</b>\n2. <b>1939</b> (23 August)\n3. (i) <b>Germany</b> (ii) <b>the Soviet Union</b>'},
{n:'B4.',q:'Why did they sign the pact?',a:'Hitler wanted to invade Poland without a two-front war against the USSR. Stalin did not trust Britain and France (excluded from Munich), needed time to rebuild the Red Army, and gained eastern Poland and the Baltic states (secret protocol).'},
{n:'B5.',q:'How the pact contributed to the outbreak of WWII.',a:'It removed the threat of Soviet opposition, so Hitler invaded Poland on 1 September 1939. Britain and France had guaranteed Poland, so they declared war on 3 September 1939.'},
{n:'B6.',q:'How the pact ended.',a:'Germany broke it by invading the USSR in <b>Operation Barbarossa</b> on 22 June 1941.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: why Britain and France followed appeasement; how appeasement failed.',a:'<b>Why:</b>\n• Horror of WWI and strong public desire for peace.\n• Many felt Versailles was too harsh, so some German demands seemed fair.\n• Britain and France were not ready for war: rearmament was slow and the Depression limited money.\n• Fear of communism (Hitler was a barrier against Stalin); the Dominions and the USA would not help.\n<b>Failures:</b>\n• Rhineland (1936) and Anschluss (1938) went unpunished, so Hitler grew bolder.\n• Munich (September 1938): the Sudetenland was given away; Chamberlain claimed "peace for our time", but Hitler took the rest of Czechoslovakia in March 1939.\n• It alienated Stalin, leading to the Nazi-Soviet Pact; Hitler expected no action over Poland.\n<b>Conclusion:</b> appeasement was understandable, but it convinced Hitler the Allies were weak, so war came in September 1939 on worse terms.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Gandhi and modern India (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Name of the mass struggle in the resolution.',a:'The <b>Quit India Movement</b> (1942)'},
{n:'A2.',q:'Formation of the Congress Party.',a:'The Indian National Congress was formed in 1885 (with the help of A.O. Hume) by educated Indians, to build national unity and represent Indian views to the British rulers. It later became the main independence movement.'},
{n:'A3.',q:'A word describing Congress\'s opinion of British rule.',a:'e.g. "imperialist", "authoritarian" or "dominating" (one word from the text).'},
{n:'A4–5.',q:'Why non-violence was favoured; why Gandhi was chosen to lead.',a:'4. Non-violence (satyagraha) matched Indian religious values, involved millions of ordinary people, gave the British no excuse for violent repression, and exposed British injustice to the world.\n5. Gandhi had huge popular support across castes and religions, had already led successful campaigns (Champaran, Non-Cooperation, the Salt March), and was respected worldwide as a moral leader.'},
{n:'B1–3.',q:'Year; the Muslim League leader; describe the event (photo).',a:'1. <b>1947</b>\n2. <b>Muhammad Ali Jinnah</b>\n3. The <b>Partition of India</b> (August 1947): British India was divided into Hindu-majority India and Muslim-majority Pakistan, causing a mass migration of about 10–15 million refugees and terrible communal violence.'},
{n:'B4.',q:'One cause of the event.',a:'Hindu–Muslim tension and the Muslim League\'s demand for a separate Muslim state (the Lahore Resolution 1940; Direct Action Day 1946). The Muslims feared domination by a Hindu-majority Congress government, and Britain wanted a quick exit.'},
{n:'B5.',q:'Gandhi\'s reaction.',a:'Gandhi strongly opposed partition ("vivisection of India"). He refused to celebrate independence and instead toured riot areas (Noakhali, Calcutta) and fasted to stop the killing between Hindus and Muslims.'},
{n:'B6.',q:'One outcome.',a:'Up to 1 million people were killed and millions became refugees. India and Pakistan became enemies, with a lasting dispute over Kashmir. Gandhi was assassinated in January 1948.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: three tactics Gandhi used; three events that let him take credit for independence.',a:'<b>Tactics:</b>\n1. Satyagraha and non-violent civil disobedience, e.g. the <b>Salt March</b> (1930).\n2. Non-cooperation and boycotts (1920–22): boycotting British cloth, schools and courts; spinning khadi; hartals (strikes, e.g. against the Rowlatt Act).\n3. Fasting, prayer and the simple life: wearing a dhoti and appealing to all castes and religions to unite.\n<b>Events:</b>\n1. The Non-Cooperation Movement (1920) made Congress a mass movement.\n2. The Salt March (1930) gained world sympathy, and Gandhi attended the Round Table Conference (1931).\n3. Quit India (1942) showed the British they could no longer govern India against its will. After WWII a weakened Britain granted independence (1947).\n<b>Conclusion:</b> other factors (WWII, Nehru, Jinnah, Labour\'s 1945 victory) mattered, but Gandhi united the masses and won moral authority, so he is seen as the "Father of the Nation".'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – The USA, 1920–1945 (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Figure seated at the desk.',a:'<b>President Franklin D. Roosevelt</b>'},
{n:'A2–3.',q:'The American view of the outbreak of WWII; this person\'s view.',a:'2. Americans were divided: isolationists ("Keep out of war") wanted to avoid another European war, while interventionists wanted to help the Allies.\n3. Roosevelt was the leading interventionist: he believed that Nazi aggression threatened US security and democracy, so the USA should help Britain and France ("arsenal of democracy").'},
{n:'A4.',q:'Why the men with the posters wanted the USA to keep out of war.',a:'They remembered the heavy losses and disappointments of WWI (the Nye Committee blamed bankers and arms makers), wanted to fix problems at home after the Depression, and believed the oceans protected America (isolationism, the America First Committee).'},
{n:'A5.',q:'The event and year that brought the USA into WWII.',a:'Event: the Japanese attack on <b>Pearl Harbor</b>\nYear: <b>1941</b> (7 December)'},
{n:'B1–3.',q:'Living standards in the 1920s; one advantage; the term for producing large quantities.',a:'1. Living standards rose rapidly: more people owned cars, radios, refrigerators and washing machines (a consumer society).\n2. Life became easier and more comfortable: labour-saving devices, more leisure, mobility from cars.\n3. <b>Mass production</b>'},
{n:'B4–5.',q:'The federal policy behind the wealth; a reason for rapid growth.',a:'4. <b>Laissez-faire</b> (little government interference), low taxes on the rich and businesses, and high protective tariffs (Fordney-McCumber 1922) that kept out foreign goods.\n5. Mass production on assembly lines (Ford) lowered prices, while hire purchase (credit) and advertising increased demand.'},
{n:'B6.',q:'How the prosperity ended.',a:'Overproduction, falling demand, unequal wealth and risky speculation on credit led to the <b>Wall Street Crash (October 1929)</b>. Share prices collapsed, banks failed and businesses closed, causing the Great Depression (about 25% unemployment by 1933).'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: three US foreign policies 1939–41; why they were abandoned 1941–45.',a:'<b>Policies:</b>\n1. Neutrality and isolationism (Neutrality Acts 1935–37): no loans or arms to nations at war.\n2. <b>Cash and Carry</b> (November 1939): the Allies could buy arms if they paid cash and carried them in their own ships.\n3. <b>Lend-Lease</b> (March 1941): weapons lent or leased to Britain (and later the USSR), making the USA the "arsenal of democracy". (Also: the destroyers-for-bases deal 1940, the Atlantic Charter 1941.)\n<b>Why abandoned:</b>\n• Pearl Harbor (7 December 1941): Japan attacked, so the USA declared war; Germany and Italy declared war on the USA (11 December).\n• Neutrality could no longer protect the USA; full involvement was needed to defeat the Axis (Europe first, then the Pacific).\n• The USA moved to total war: conscription, mass war production, D-Day (1944), the atomic bombs (1945).\n<b>Conclusion:</b> isolationism gradually weakened from 1939 and ended completely in December 1941.'}
]},
{t:'Q6 – Japan, 1912–1945 (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1–2.',q:'A feature showing Japanese power in the Far East; how WWI helped Japan\'s economy.',a:'1. Japan joined the Allies and seized Germany\'s Pacific islands and Shandong in China, and pressed the <b>Twenty-One Demands</b> on China (1915).\n2. With the European powers busy fighting, Japan took over their Asian markets. Exports of textiles, ships and munitions boomed, giving huge profits and industrial growth.'},
{n:'A3.',q:'Why the army became more powerful than the government.',a:'Under the Meiji constitution the army answered directly to the Emperor, not to parliament, and the army minister had to be a serving officer, so the army could bring down governments. Nationalism, the Depression and the assassination of politicians by extremist officers increased its power.'},
{n:'A4.',q:'Japanese policy 1914–1918.',a:'Japan joined WWI on the Allied side (Anglo-Japanese Alliance 1902), took German territories in China and the Pacific, issued the Twenty-One Demands to dominate China, and sent troops to Siberia (1918), expanding its influence in East Asia.'},
{n:'A5.',q:'Year all adult men got the vote.',a:'<b>1925</b>'},
{n:'B1–2.',q:'The event behind the radio address; the country Japan surrendered to.',a:'1. The <b>atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki</b> (August 1945) led to Emperor Hirohito\'s surrender broadcast (15 August 1945).\n2. The <b>USA</b> (the Allies)'},
{n:'B3–4.',q:'Evidence of the impact on the people; why they were sad.',a:'3. The people are kneeling and bowing with heads down, some weeping, showing grief and shock.\n4. It was the first time most had heard the Emperor\'s voice. He announced defeat and surrender after years of sacrifice, and many felt shame and loss of honour.'},
{n:'B5–6.',q:'Activity that prompted the address; one consequence.',a:'5. Japan\'s war of expansion in Asia and the Pacific (WWII), including the attack on Pearl Harbor and the occupation of the Pacific islands such as Tarawa.\n6. The US occupation (1945–52) under General MacArthur: Japan lost its empire and army, and a new democratic constitution (1947) made the Emperor only a symbol and renounced war (Article 9).'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: Japan\'s military successes 1912–1930s; what hindered them in the 1940s.',a:'<b>Successes:</b>\n• WWI gains: Shandong and the German Pacific islands (1914), the Twenty-One Demands (1915), a League of Nations mandate (1919).\n• The Manchurian Incident (1931) → the puppet state of Manchukuo; Japan left the League (1933).\n• Full-scale war with China (1937): the capture of Shanghai and Nanjing.\n• 1941–42: Pearl Harbor, then rapid conquest of the Philippines, Malaya, Singapore, the Dutch East Indies and the Gilbert Islands (Tarawa).\n<b>Hindrances in the 1940s:</b>\n• The US entered the war with far greater industrial power.\n• Midway (June 1942) destroyed four carriers; defeat at Guadalcanal (1943).\n• Island hopping (the Battle of Tarawa, November 1943), submarines cutting oil and supplies, firebombing of cities.\n• The atomic bombs (August 1945) and the Soviet declaration of war → surrender.\n<b>Conclusion:</b> early successes rested on surprise and weak opponents, but Japan could not match American resources in a long war.'}
]}
]},
{y:2024, src:'P2024/2024 KSSC History Exam.pdf', note:'Answer Q1 (compulsory) plus TWO of Q2–Q6. These are model answers in plain words, with an essay plan for each Part C: use the plan (intro → 3 paragraphs → conclusion) and add dates and names.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – The search for security in the nuclear age, 1945–1990 (50 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Name the person in the cartoon (Truman Doctrine cartoon)',a:'<b>Joseph Stalin</b>, the Soviet leader. (Truman is the US President who announced the doctrine.)'},
{n:'A3.',q:'Impact of the Truman Doctrine on the Soviet leader',a:'Stalin saw it as a <b>direct threat</b>: the USA was promising to stop the spread of communism anywhere. It made him <b>tighten control over Eastern Europe</b> (Cominform 1947, the Czech coup 1948) and was a step towards the <b>Berlin Blockade</b>. Cartoon evidence: Stalin is shown blocked, angry or worried by Truman\'s action.'},
{n:'A4.',q:'Two reasons for the Truman Doctrine (1947)',a:'1. Communist guerrillas threatened to take over <b>Greece</b>, and the USSR was pressuring <b>Turkey</b>. Britain could no longer afford to help them.\n2. The USA wanted to <b>contain communism</b> (Kennan\'s containment idea) and protect democracy and trade (the domino fear).'},
{n:'A5.',q:'Why the character would feel threatened',a:'The USA promised <b>money and military aid</b> to any country resisting communism, which surrounded the USSR with US-backed states. Together with the Marshall Plan and the US atomic monopoly, Stalin felt the USA was trying to <b>encircle and weaken the Soviet Union</b>.'},
{n:'B1.',q:'One problem in Berlin that led to the Wall',a:'A <b>mass escape of East Germans</b> to the West through West Berlin: about 2.5–3 million left between 1949 and 1961, many of them skilled young workers. This was a "brain drain" that hurt East Germany\'s economy and embarrassed communism.'},
{n:'B2.',q:'One reason from the text why the Wall was built',a:'To <b>stop free contact with the Western world</b> and stop East Germans fleeing to the West.'},
{n:'B3.',q:'"A gap in the system existed in Berlin"',a:'The rest of the border between East and West Germany was sealed, but in <b>Berlin people could simply cross</b> from the Soviet sector into the Western sectors. Berlin was the one <b>"hole" in the Iron Curtain</b> through which East Germans could escape.'},
{n:'B5.',q:'Impact of the Berlin Wall on Cold War tensions',a:'In the short term it <b>reduced the immediate crisis</b>: Kennedy said "a wall is better than a war", and the refugee flow stopped. But it became the <b>symbol of the Cold War</b> and the "Iron Curtain". There was a tank stand-off at Checkpoint Charlie (Oct 1961), Kennedy\'s "Ich bin ein Berliner" speech (1963), and deaths of people trying to escape. It <b>deepened mistrust</b> between East and West until it fell in <b>1989</b>.'},
{n:'C1.',q:'Year of the Korean incident',a:'<b>1950</b> (North Korea invaded on 25 June 1950).'},
{n:'C2.',q:'Two countries on either side',a:'<b>North Korea</b> (backed by the USSR and China) and <b>South Korea</b> (backed by the USA and the UN).'},
{n:'C3.',q:'Official status of a superpower in Korea',a:'The <b>USA fought officially under the United Nations flag</b>. The UN Security Council voted to send troops while the USSR was boycotting it, and US General MacArthur was the UN commander. The USSR was <b>not officially at war</b>; it only supplied weapons and pilots secretly.'},
{n:'C5.',q:'Three events in the Korean War up to 1953',a:'1. <b>June 1950</b>: North Korea invades and pushes South Korea back to Pusan.\n2. <b>Sept 1950</b>: the UN landing at <b>Inchon</b> pushes the North back to the Chinese border (the Yalu River).\n3. <b>Oct–Nov 1950</b>: <b>China</b> enters the war and pushes the UN back south. 1951: MacArthur is sacked. <b>July 1953</b>: armistice at Panmunjom.'},
{n:'C6.',q:'Result of the war',a:'Korea stayed <b>divided at about the 38th parallel</b> (a demilitarised zone), and no peace treaty was ever signed. About 3 million people died and both Koreas were devastated. Containment "worked" because South Korea stayed non-communist, and China gained prestige.'},
{n:'C7.',q:'How the war affected superpower relations',a:'It made the Cold War <b>global and military</b>. The USA tripled its defence spending, built alliances (SEATO 1954), and rearmed West Germany within NATO. The USSR answered with the Warsaw Pact (1955). Mistrust and the <b>arms race</b> grew, and the USA now saw China as an enemy too.'},
{n:'D (essay).',q:'Explain the ideologies of the USA and USSR and how they shaped their foreign policy after WWII',a:'<b>Intro</b>: After 1945 the two superpowers had opposite ideologies, which led to the Cold War.\n<b>1. USA – capitalism and democracy</b>: free elections, free speech, private business and free trade. Foreign policy aimed to <b>contain communism</b> and open markets: the Truman Doctrine (1947), the Marshall Plan ($13 billion to rebuild Europe), NATO (1949), and the Korean War.\n<b>2. USSR – communism</b>: one-party state, state ownership, no private profit, and a belief in world revolution. Stalin wanted <b>security</b> after 27 million deaths, so he created a <b>buffer zone</b> of satellite states in Eastern Europe (the "Iron Curtain"), Cominform/Comecon, the Berlin Blockade, and the Warsaw Pact (1955).\n<b>3. Clash</b>: each side saw the other\'s policy as aggressive. This led to an arms race (Soviet A-bomb 1949), crises (Berlin, Cuba 1962), and proxy wars.\n<b>Conclusion</b>: their ideologies made each side fear and oppose the other, shaping 45 years of foreign policy until 1990.'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Causes of World War I, 1900–1914 (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1–3.',q:'Alliance in the Bismarck cartoon; year created',a:'The <b>Triple Alliance</b> (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy), formed in <b>1882</b>. It grew out of the Dual Alliance of 1879.'},
{n:'A4.',q:'Political reason for Bismarck\'s alliance system',a:'To <b>isolate France</b> so it could never find allies for a war of revenge, and to keep peace in Europe so the new German Empire (1871) could grow strong.'},
{n:'A5.',q:'Why Bismarck saw France as a threat',a:'France lost the Franco-Prussian War (1870–71) and the provinces of <b>Alsace-Lorraine</b> to Germany. France wanted <b>revenge (revanche)</b> and the land back, so it was Germany\'s most likely enemy.'},
{n:'A6.',q:'How alliances threatened peace',a:'Europe split into <b>two armed camps</b>: the Triple Alliance against the Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain, from 1907). A local quarrel could drag in all the great powers. That is what happened in 1914: Austria vs Serbia brought in Russia, then Germany, France and Britain within days (a <b>chain reaction</b>).'},
{n:'B1–2.',q:'Name the treaty; another signatory',a:'The <b>Treaty of London, 1839</b>. Other signatories: <b>France</b>, Austria, Russia (and Prussia, Germany\'s forerunner).'},
{n:'B3.',q:'Two conditions of the treaty',a:'1. <b>Belgium would be an independent and permanently neutral</b> state.\n2. The great powers <b>guaranteed</b> to protect Belgium\'s neutrality and not to invade it.'},
{n:'B4.',q:'Why did Germany send troops into Belgium?',a:'The <b>Schlieffen Plan</b>: to defeat France quickly by going round the strong French forts, through flat Belgium, then turning on Russia before Russia could mobilise. This avoided a long two-front war.'},
{n:'B6.',q:'Consequence of Britain going to war over a "scrap of paper"',a:'The German Chancellor called the 1839 treaty a "scrap of paper", but Britain kept its promise and <b>declared war on Germany on 4 August 1914</b>. A European war became a <b>world war</b>, bringing in the British Empire (India, Australia, NZ, Canada) and later costing millions of lives.'},
{n:'C (essay).',q:'Explain nationalism, imperialism, militarism and alliances (MAIN) as causes of WWI',a:'<b>Intro</b>: The long-term causes were MAIN; the spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand (Sarajevo, 28 June 1914).\n<b>Militarism</b>: an arms race: the naval race between Britain and Germany (Dreadnoughts from 1906), huge conscript armies, and war plans (the Schlieffen Plan) that made quick mobilisation and war likely.\n<b>Alliances</b>: Triple Alliance vs Triple Entente, two armed camps, so one conflict pulled everyone in.\n<b>Imperialism</b>: competition for colonies and markets. The Moroccan crises (1905, 1911) caused anger between Germany and France/Britain.\n<b>Nationalism</b>: Slav nationalism in the Balkans (Serbia, the Black Hand) against Austria-Hungary; French revenge over Alsace-Lorraine; German pride.\n<b>Conclusion</b>: together they created tension, so the assassination quickly became a general war.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Peace and war, 1919–1939 (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Define reparations',a:'<b>Payments for war damage</b> that a defeated country must pay. Germany was ordered to pay £6.6 billion (1921) under the Treaty of Versailles.'},
{n:'A2.',q:'One of the Big Three',a:'<b>Georges Clemenceau</b> (France), <b>David Lloyd George</b> (Britain), or <b>Woodrow Wilson</b> (USA).'},
{n:'A4.',q:'How his (Clemenceau\'s) view helped cause WWII',a:'Clemenceau wanted a <b>harsh treaty</b> to cripple Germany: huge reparations, war guilt, a tiny army and lost land. This made Germans <b>bitter and humiliated</b>, damaged their economy (hyperinflation 1923), and gave Hitler support when he promised to tear up the treaty.'},
{n:'A5.',q:'Why Germany is shown as a prisoner',a:'Germans felt the treaty was a "<b>Diktat</b>" (dictated peace): they were not allowed to negotiate, only sign or be invaded. Germany was "chained" by reparations, Article 231 (war guilt), disarmament and occupation of the Rhineland, like a prisoner being punished.'},
{n:'A6.',q:'German reactions to Versailles',a:'<b>Anger and humiliation</b>. They called it a Diktat and blamed the "November criminals" (the politicians who signed). There were uprisings (the Kapp Putsch 1920, Hitler\'s Munich Putsch 1923), and many wanted revenge and to overturn it.'},
{n:'B1–3.',q:'Nazi-Soviet Pact: year, an agreement, the countries',a:'Signed <b>23 August 1939</b> by <b>Germany</b> (Ribbentrop) and the <b>USSR</b> (Molotov).\nAgreement: <b>not to attack each other for 10 years</b>, plus a secret plan to <b>divide Poland</b> (and the Baltic states) between them.'},
{n:'B4.',q:'Why did they sign?',a:'<b>Hitler</b> wanted to invade Poland without fighting the USSR at the same time (no two-front war). <b>Stalin</b> did not trust Britain and France (after Munich 1938), wanted <b>time to build up his army</b>, and wanted land in Eastern Europe.'},
{n:'B6.',q:'How the Pact ended',a:'Hitler broke it on <b>22 June 1941</b> with <b>Operation Barbarossa</b>, the German invasion of the Soviet Union.'},
{n:'C (essay).',q:'Three attempts at collective security in the 1920s, and their weaknesses',a:'<b>Intro</b>: collective security means countries act together through the League of Nations to stop aggression.\n<b>1. The League of Nations (1920)</b>: sanctions and peace talks; it solved small disputes (the Åland Islands 1921, Greece–Bulgaria 1925). Weakness: the <b>USA never joined</b>, Germany and the USSR were left out at first, and it had <b>no army</b>.\n<b>2. The Washington Naval Conference (1921–22)</b>: limited battleships (USA:Britain:Japan = 5:5:3). Weakness: no enforcement; Japan felt insulted and later broke it.\n<b>3. The Locarno Treaties (1925)</b> and the <b>Kellogg-Briand Pact (1928)</b>: Germany accepted its western borders, and 65 nations promised not to use war. Weakness: no punishment for breaking them, and Germany\'s eastern borders were not guaranteed.\n<b>Evaluate</b>: they relied on goodwill. In the 1930s Depression, Japan (Manchuria 1931), Italy (Abyssinia 1935) and Germany ignored them, and collective security collapsed.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Gandhi and modern India, 1915–1947 (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Name of the mass struggle in the resolution',a:'<b>Satyagraha</b> / the <b>Non-Cooperation Movement</b> (1920–22).'},
{n:'A2.',q:'Why was the Congress Party formed?',a:'The Indian National Congress was founded in <b>1885</b> to give educated Indians a <b>voice in government</b> and to ask Britain for more rights. It later became the main movement for <b>independence (swaraj)</b>.'},
{n:'A5.',q:'Why was Gandhi chosen to lead?',a:'He had successfully led <b>non-violent campaigns</b> in South Africa and in India (Champaran 1917, Kheda 1918). He was respected by ordinary villagers, and his methods could bring <b>millions of Indians together</b>, Hindus and Muslims, rich and poor.'},
{n:'B1–2.',q:'Year of the event; the Muslim League leader',a:'Year: <b>1946/1947</b> (Direct Action Day riots, August 1946, and the Partition violence of 1947).\nLeader: <b>Muhammad Ali Jinnah</b>.'},
{n:'B3.',q:'Describe the event',a:'<b>Communal violence and Partition</b>. When British India was divided into India and Pakistan (August 1947), Hindus, Sikhs and Muslims attacked each other. About <b>10–15 million people were forced to move</b> and up to 1 million were killed.'},
{n:'B5.',q:'Gandhi\'s reaction',a:'Gandhi was <b>heartbroken</b>. He opposed Partition and went to riot areas (Noakhali, Calcutta), <b>fasting</b> until the violence stopped, and pleaded for Hindu–Muslim unity. He was assassinated on 30 Jan 1948 by a Hindu extremist.'},
{n:'B6.',q:'One outcome',a:'<b>Creation of two states, India and Pakistan</b> (15 August 1947), and lasting hostility between them (e.g. the Kashmir dispute).'},
{n:'C (essay).',q:'Three tactics Gandhi used to bring about independence',a:'<b>Intro</b>: Gandhi\'s method was satyagraha ("truth-force"): non-violent resistance.\n<b>1. Non-Cooperation (1920–22)</b>: boycott British cloth, schools, courts and titles, and spin your own cloth (khadi). It showed India\'s unity. It was called off after violence at Chauri Chaura.\n<b>2. Civil disobedience – the Salt March (1930)</b>: a 388 km walk to Dandi to make salt, breaking the British salt tax law. About 60 000 people were arrested, and it got world attention. Britain invited Gandhi to the Round Table Conference (1931).\n<b>3. Quit India Movement (1942)</b>: a mass demand that Britain leave immediately. Leaders were jailed, but it showed Britain could not rule India after the war.\nAlso: <b>fasting</b> and <b>hartals</b> (strikes).\n<b>Conclusion</b>: non-violence won world sympathy, united Indians and wore down British rule, leading to independence in 1947.'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – The USA, 1920–1945 (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Figure at the desk',a:'<b>President Franklin D. Roosevelt</b> (FDR).'},
{n:'A2.',q:'American view of WWII shown in the cartoon',a:'Most Americans were <b>isolationist</b>: they wanted to stay <b>neutral</b> and keep out of Europe\'s war (remembering WWI losses), as shown by the Neutrality Acts of 1935–37.'},
{n:'A4.',q:'Why did the men with posters want the USA to stay out?',a:'They feared <b>American soldiers would die again</b> as in WWI, and believed the war was Europe\'s problem. Groups like the "America First" committee said the USA was safe behind two oceans.'},
{n:'A5.',q:'Event that brought the USA into WWII',a:'Japan\'s attack on <b>Pearl Harbor, 7 December 1941</b>.'},
{n:'B1–2.',q:'Living standards in the 1920s; two advantages',a:'Living standards were <b>rising fast</b> (the "Roaring Twenties"): more people owned cars, radios and electrical goods.\nAdvantages: 1. <b>Easier lives</b>, with labour-saving devices (washing machines, vacuum cleaners). 2. <b>More leisure and travel</b> (cars, cinema, radio), and more jobs in the new industries.'},
{n:'B4.',q:'Federal policy of the 1920s that helped growth',a:'<b>Laissez-faire</b> (little government interference in business), <b>low taxes</b> (Mellon), and <b>high tariffs</b> (Fordney–McCumber Act 1922) to protect American industry from foreign competition.'},
{n:'B5.',q:'A reason for rapid growth',a:'<b>Mass production</b> (Henry Ford\'s assembly line made the Model T cheap), plus <b>hire purchase / credit</b> that let people buy now and pay later, and advertising.'},
{n:'B6.',q:'How prosperity ended',a:'<b>The Wall Street Crash, October 1929</b>. Share prices collapsed, banks failed and businesses closed, leading to the <b>Great Depression</b>: about 13 million unemployed (25%) by 1933. Causes: overproduction, too much credit and speculation, and unequal wealth.'},
{n:'C (essay).',q:'Three US foreign policies during WWII, 1939–1941',a:'<b>Intro</b>: The USA moved from strict neutrality to helping the Allies before entering the war.\n<b>1. Cash and Carry (Nov 1939)</b>: countries at war could buy US arms if they paid cash and carried them in their own ships. This helped Britain and France while the USA stayed out.\n<b>2. Destroyers-for-Bases deal (Sept 1940)</b>: 50 old destroyers given to Britain in exchange for leases on British bases.\n<b>3. Lend-Lease Act (March 1941)</b>: FDR made the USA the "arsenal of democracy", lending weapons and supplies (later also to the USSR) without immediate payment. Also the Atlantic Charter (Aug 1941) with Churchill, and oil and steel embargoes on Japan (1941).\n<b>Conclusion</b>: these policies supported the Allies, angered Germany and Japan, and led to Pearl Harbor and full US entry in December 1941.'}
]},
{t:'Q6 – Japan, 1912–1945 (35 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Two features showing Japan was powerful',a:'e.g. a <b>strong navy and army</b> (it defeated Russia in 1905), <b>colonies</b> (Korea, Taiwan), and growing industry and trade.'},
{n:'A2.',q:'How WWI helped Japan\'s economy',a:'European countries were busy fighting, so Japan <b>took over their Asian markets</b> and supplied the Allies with ships and goods. Its exports and industry boomed, and it became a creditor nation.'},
{n:'A4.',q:'Japanese policy 1914–1918',a:'Japan joined the Allies and <b>seized German colonies</b> (Shandong/Tsingtao in China, and Pacific islands including the Marshalls and Carolines). In 1915 it made the <b>Twenty-One Demands</b> on China to gain control there.'},
{n:'A5.',q:'Year all adult men got the vote',a:'<b>1925</b> (the Universal Manhood Suffrage Law).'},
{n:'B1–2.',q:'Event that prompted the Emperor\'s radio address; to whom did Japan surrender?',a:'The <b>atomic bombings of Hiroshima (6 Aug) and Nagasaki (9 Aug) 1945</b>, plus the Soviet declaration of war. Emperor Hirohito\'s broadcast came on 15 August 1945.\nJapan surrendered to the <b>Allies, led by the United States</b> (signed 2 Sept 1945 on USS Missouri).'},
{n:'B3.',q:'Two pieces of photo evidence of the impact',a:'e.g. <b>people bowing or weeping</b> while listening (shock and grief); <b>ruined buildings</b> in the background. Answer from the details you can see in the photo.'},
{n:'B5.',q:'Activity that led to the broadcast',a:'Japan\'s <b>war in the Pacific</b>: its expansion and refusal to surrender (it rejected the Potsdam Declaration, July 1945) after attacking Pearl Harbor and occupying South-East Asia and the Pacific, including Tarawa (1941–43).'},
{n:'B6.',q:'One consequence for the Japanese',a:'<b>US occupation (1945–52)</b> under MacArthur. Japan lost its empire, was disarmed, and got a new democratic constitution (1947) that gave up war (Article 9). The Emperor lost his political power.'},
{n:'C (essay).',q:'Japan\'s military successes 1912–1930s, and what hindered them in the 1940s',a:'<b>Successes</b>: took German colonies in WWI (1914); the Twenty-One Demands on China (1915); invaded <b>Manchuria in 1931</b> (the puppet state of Manchukuo) and left the League when criticised; full-scale <b>war with China from 1937</b> (Shanghai, Nanjing); 1941–42 Pearl Harbor, Malaya, Singapore, the Philippines, the Dutch East Indies, Kiribati (Tarawa) and the Solomons.\n<b>Hindrances in the 1940s</b>: 1. US <b>oil and steel embargo</b> (1941). 2. Defeat at <b>Midway (June 1942)</b>, where it lost 4 carriers, followed by Guadalcanal. 3. US "island hopping" (<b>Tarawa, Nov 1943</b>, Saipan, Iwo Jima, Okinawa) and the bombing of Japanese cities. 4. The <b>atomic bombs</b> and the Soviet invasion of Manchuria (Aug 1945).\n<b>Conclusion</b>: Japan was strong against weak neighbours, but could not match US industrial power, which led to surrender in 1945.'}
]}
]},
{y:2023, src:'P2023/HISTORY EXAM 2023.pdf', note:'Model answers based on the MOE marking scheme. Question 1 is compulsory, then answer any TWO of Questions 2–6. Read each resource in the paper alongside the answer. The essays are given as plans: write them out in full paragraphs with an introduction and a conclusion.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Search for security in the nuclear age, 1945–1990 (compulsory)', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'1962 cartoon: two men; crisis; cause; result; effect on relations',a:'1. <b>Nikita Khrushchev</b> (USSR) and <b>John F. Kennedy</b> (USA).\n2. The <b>Cuban Missile Crisis</b> (October 1962).\n3. US U-2 spy planes found the USSR <b>installing nuclear missiles in Cuba</b>, only about 145 km (90 miles) from Florida, after the failed US Bay of Pigs invasion and US missiles placed in Turkey.\n4. After 13 days of US naval blockade, Khrushchev <b>removed the missiles</b>; the USA promised <b>not to invade Cuba</b> and secretly removed its missiles from Turkey.\n5. Both sides realised how close they came to nuclear war, so they set up the <b>hotline</b> between Washington and Moscow (1963) and signed the <b>Partial (Limited) Test Ban Treaty</b> (1963) – the start of a slow move towards détente.'},
{n:'B1–5.',q:'Containment; communism; loss of China; 1949 change; "soft on communism"; Korea',a:'1. Containment: the US policy (Truman Doctrine 1947, George Kennan) of stopping communism spreading to new countries using aid and military force. Communism: a system where the state owns land and industry, there is one party, and wealth is meant to be shared equally.\n2. China was the world\'s most populous country; the US had backed Chiang Kai-shek with huge aid, so the communist victory greatly increased the communist bloc and looked like a failure of US policy in Asia.\n3. In 1949 Mao Zedong\'s Communists defeated the Nationalists (who fled to Taiwan) and set up the <b>People\'s Republic of China</b> – a one-party communist state.\n4. Republican critics (e.g. Senator McCarthy) said Truman had not done enough to stop communism in China and at home; this pressure pushed Truman to act firmly in Korea.\n5. <b>25 June 1950</b>.'},
{n:'C1–6.',q:'1974 photo: leaders; economic reason; why improve relations; example; why it didn\'t last; the term',a:'1. <b>Richard Nixon</b> (USA) and <b>Leonid Brezhnev</b> (USSR).\n2. The arms race was too costly; the US wanted trade (e.g. selling grain to the USSR) to help an economy hit by inflation and the Vietnam War.\n3. (i) To reduce the risk of <b>nuclear war</b>. (ii) To cut the huge <b>cost of the arms race</b> (and both wanted trade and technology).\n4. <b>SALT I</b> (1972) limited nuclear missiles; also the Apollo–Soyuz space link-up (1975) and the Helsinki Agreement (1975).\n5. They still did not trust each other and kept building weapons; the USSR <b>invaded Afghanistan in 1979</b>, so the US refused to ratify SALT II and boycotted the 1980 Moscow Olympics.\n6. <b>Détente</b> – French for "relaxation", meaning an easing of tension between the superpowers in the 1970s, e.g. SALT I and Nixon\'s visit to Moscow in 1972.'},
{n:'D.',q:'Essay: three Cold War crises 1950–1968 and how each increased tension',a:'Plan:\nIntro: the Cold War was rivalry between the USA and USSR without direct fighting; crises brought them close to war.\n1. <b>Korean War (1950–53)</b>: North Korea (backed by USSR/China) invaded the South; a UN force led by the USA fought back; China entered when MacArthur advanced to the Yalu. → Tension rose as the superpowers backed opposite sides in a real war; Korea stayed divided.\n2. <b>Berlin Wall (1961)</b>: millions fled East Germany through Berlin; the USSR and East Germany built the wall overnight. → US and Soviet tanks faced each other at Checkpoint Charlie; the wall became the symbol of the divided world.\n3. <b>Cuban Missile Crisis (1962)</b>: Soviet missiles in Cuba; US blockade. → The closest the world came to nuclear war.\n(Also: U-2 incident 1960; Hungary 1956; Prague Spring 1968.)\nConclusion: each crisis increased fear and the arms race, though Cuba also pushed them to seek ways to reduce tension.'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Causes of World War I', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'Schlieffen Plan',a:'1. <b>Helmuth von Moltke</b> (the Younger). He weakened the right wing (sent troops to the Russian front and Alsace) and turned the attack <b>east of Paris</b> instead of sweeping around west of it.\n2. (i) Defeat France quickly (in about 6 weeks) by attacking through <b>Belgium</b>. (ii) Then move troops by rail to fight <b>Russia</b> before it fully mobilised – avoiding a two-front war.\n3. Because the plan needed speed, Germany had to declare war on France as soon as Russia mobilised, and invading neutral Belgium brought <b>Britain</b> into the war (Treaty of London 1839) – turning a Balkan quarrel into a European war.\n4. <b>Belgian resistance</b> (e.g. at Liège) delayed the advance, and the British Expeditionary Force arrived; Russia also mobilised faster than expected.\n5. The plan failed at the <b>Battle of the Marne</b> (Sept 1914): the exhausted German army was pushed back and dug trenches, so Germany was stuck in a long two-front war.'},
{n:'B1–5.',q:'Sarajevo assassination',a:'1. <b>Archduke Franz Ferdinand</b>, heir to the throne of <b>Austria-Hungary</b>.\n2. Serbian nationalists wanted Bosnia (annexed by Austria in 1908) to join a greater Serbia/Yugoslavia; killing the heir was a protest against Austrian rule.\n3. The <b>Black Hand</b> (Unification or Death); the assassin was Gavrilo Princip.\n4. Austria blamed Serbia and, backed by Germany (the "blank cheque"), sent an ultimatum and declared war on 28 July 1914; Russia mobilised to support Serbia, Germany declared war on Russia and France, and Britain joined when Belgium was invaded – the alliance system turned it into a world war.\n5. The Black Hand was led by Serbian army officers (Colonel Apis) and the weapons came from Serbia; Austria said the Serbian government knew about the plot.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: three alliances 1879–1907; why they failed',a:'Plan:\n1. <b>Dual Alliance (1879)</b> Germany–Austria-Hungary, which became the <b>Triple Alliance (1882)</b> with Italy.\n2. <b>Franco-Russian Alliance (1894)</b>.\n3. <b>Entente Cordiale (1904)</b> Britain–France, and the <b>Anglo-Russian Entente (1907)</b> → the Triple Entente.\nThey were meant to keep peace by balancing power.\nWhy they failed: they split Europe into two armed camps; promises to help allies meant a local conflict (Sarajevo) drew in all the great powers; they encouraged the arms race and military plans like Schlieffen; Germany felt encircled. Italy left its alliance, showing alliances were unreliable.\nConclusion: instead of preventing war, the alliances guaranteed it would be large.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Origins of World War II', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'Treaty of Versailles',a:'1. The 1919 peace treaty between the Allies and Germany ending WWI.\n2. Germans called it a <b>"diktat"</b> (dictated peace); they were angry at the war-guilt clause (Article 231), reparations (£6600 million), loss of land and the tiny army.\n3. (i) <b>Rearmament</b> and conscription from 1935. (ii) <b>Remilitarisation of the Rhineland</b> (1936). (Also Anschluss with Austria, 1938.)\n4. France wanted a harsh treaty to keep Germany weak and safe from invasion (Clemenceau); many French felt it was still not harsh enough.\n5. German bitterness helped Hitler win support by promising to destroy it; when he broke its terms, Britain and France did nothing, encouraging further aggression.'},
{n:'B1–6.',q:'Appeasement and Munich',a:'1. Giving in to reasonable demands of an aggressor to avoid war.\n2. <b>Neville Chamberlain</b> (British PM).\n3. For: Britain was not ready for war; people remembered WWI; many thought the Versailles terms were unfair. Against: it encouraged Hitler to demand more; it abandoned Czechoslovakia; it gave Germany time to rearm.\n4. It ended when Hitler took the rest of Czechoslovakia in <b>March 1939</b>; Britain and France then guaranteed Poland and declared war when Germany invaded it on 1 September 1939.\n5. It convinced Hitler that Britain and France would never fight, so he invaded Poland after the Nazi–Soviet Pact.\n6. Czechoslovakia had to hand over the <b>Sudetenland</b> and its western defences to Germany, without the Czechs or USSR being asked.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: collective security in the 1920s; weaknesses',a:'Plan:\n1. <b>League of Nations (1920)</b> – members would act together against an aggressor.\n2. <b>Locarno Treaties (1925)</b> – Germany accepted its western borders.\n3. <b>Kellogg–Briand Pact (1928)</b> – 65 countries renounced war. (Also Washington Naval Conference 1921–22.)\nWeaknesses: the USA never joined the League, and Germany and the USSR were left out at first; the League had no army and decisions needed everyone to agree; sanctions were weak. The pacts had no way to enforce them.\nResult: in the 1930s the League failed over Manchuria (1931) and Abyssinia (1935), so aggressors were encouraged, leading to WWII.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Gandhi and modern India', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'Congress resolution',a:'1. The <b>Quit India</b> movement (1942).\n2. Congress (founded 1885) was set up so educated Indians could express their views to the British and gain a greater say in government.\n3. Words from the text such as "imperialist", "authoritarian" and "dominating" (use the exact words in the resource).\n4. Gandhi taught that non-violence (<b>satyagraha</b>) was morally right and would show the injustice of British rule, win world sympathy and unite all Indians; violence would give the British an excuse to crush them.\n5. Gandhi was respected by the masses, had led successful satyagraha campaigns (South Africa, Champaran, Salt March) and could unite people of all castes and religions.'},
{n:'B1–6.',q:'Partition photo (refugee trains)',a:'1. <b>1947</b>.\n2. <b>Muhammad Ali Jinnah</b>.\n3. Refugees crowded inside and on top of trains, fleeing across the new border – Muslims to Pakistan, Hindus and Sikhs to India.\n4. The <b>Partition</b> of India into India and Pakistan (Radcliffe Line split Punjab and Bengal), because the Muslim League demanded a separate Muslim state.\n5. Gandhi opposed Partition; he went to the riot areas (Calcutta, Delhi) and <b>fasted</b> until the violence stopped, which brought temporary calm.\n6. Terrible communal violence: about 10–15 million people were displaced and up to 1 million killed; Gandhi himself was assassinated in 1948.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: Gandhi\'s tactics; events that earned him credit',a:'Plan:\nTactics: <b>satyagraha</b> (truth-force, non-violent resistance – Champaran 1917); <b>non-cooperation</b> (1920–22: boycotting British goods, schools and courts; spinning khadi); <b>civil disobedience</b> (Salt March 1930); hartals (strikes) and fasting.\nEvents: the <b>Salt March</b> (1930) won world attention and led to the Round Table talks; the <b>Quit India</b> campaign (1942) showed the British they could not rule India after the war; his leadership united Hindus, Muslims and untouchables and his fasts stopped violence.\nConclusion: although WWII weakened Britain and others (Nehru, Jinnah) played parts, Gandhi\'s mass non-violent movement made British rule impossible, so he is called the Father of the Nation.'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – USA 1920–1945', qs:[
{n:'A1–6.',q:'Hooverville',a:'1. The <b>Great Depression</b>.\n2. The <b>Wall Street Crash</b> (October 1929).\n3. Mass <b>unemployment</b> and homelessness – people living in shacks and searching for food.\n4. Shanty towns of homeless, unemployed people, built from scrap, named mockingly after President Hoover.\n5. Very poor: no running water or toilets, little food, disease, makeshift shelters.\n6. Hoover believed in <b>laissez-faire</b> and "rugged individualism"; he thought prosperity was "just around the corner" and refused large federal relief, so the problem grew.'},
{n:'B1–6.',q:'Prosperity of the 1920s',a:'1. Rising living standards – more people owned cars, radios and electrical goods ("the Roaring Twenties").\n2. (i) More leisure and comfort (radios, cinemas, cars). (ii) Labour-saving goods and better jobs/wages.\n3. <b>Mass production</b> (assembly line – Henry Ford).\n4. Republican <b>laissez-faire</b> policy: low taxes, little regulation of business, and high tariffs (Fordney–McCumber, 1922) to protect US industry.\n5. New technology and mass production made goods cheap; easy <b>credit/hire purchase</b>; electricity; the boom in car making.\n6. Overproduction, unequal wealth and speculation on shares led to the <b>Wall Street Crash</b> in 1929, banks failing and the Depression.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: US foreign policies 1939–41; why abandoned 1941–45',a:'Plan:\n1. <b>Isolationism/neutrality</b> – Neutrality Acts (1935–37) banned arms sales to warring countries.\n2. <b>Cash and Carry</b> (1939) – Britain and France could buy arms if they paid cash and carried them in their own ships.\n3. <b>Lend-Lease</b> (1941) – the USA lent weapons to Britain (and later the USSR): "the arsenal of democracy".\nWhy abandoned: Japan\'s attack on <b>Pearl Harbor</b> (7 Dec 1941) brought the USA into the war; Germany then declared war on the USA; the USA fought fully with the Allies until victory in 1945 and helped found the UN – ending isolationism.'}
]},
{t:'Q6 – Japan 1912–1945', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'Japan\'s power, WWI, the army',a:'1. (Use the text.) e.g. a strong modern <b>navy</b> and army that defeated China (1895) and Russia (1905); colonies such as Korea and Taiwan; the Anglo-Japanese Alliance.\n2. WWI cut off European goods, so Japan took over Asian markets – exports and shipbuilding boomed (e.g. textile sales to Asia).\n3. Under the constitution the army and navy ministers were serving officers responsible only to the <b>Emperor</b>; the army could bring down governments, and nationalists assassinated politicians who opposed them.\n4. Japan joined the Allies and seized German colonies in China (Shandong) and the Pacific, and made the <b>Twenty-One Demands</b> (1915) to control China.\n5. <b>1925</b> (all adult men).'},
{n:'B1–6.',q:'Emperor\'s surrender broadcast 1945',a:'1. The <b>atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki</b> (6 and 9 August 1945).\n2. The Allies, mainly the <b>USA</b>.\n3. (Use the photo.) People bowing/kneeling and weeping while listening to the radio.\n4. It was the first time they had heard the Emperor\'s voice, and he announced <b>defeat</b> – a great shame after years of sacrifice.\n5. Japan\'s war of expansion in Asia and the Pacific – the attack on Pearl Harbor and the Pacific War.\n6. US <b>occupation</b> (General MacArthur); Japan lost its empire, the army was disbanded and a new democratic constitution (1947) made the Emperor a symbol only.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: military successes 1912–1930s; what hindered them in the 1940s',a:'Plan:\nSuccesses: took German territories in China and the Pacific in WWI (1914); Twenty-One Demands (1915); invasion of <b>Manchuria</b> (1931, Manchukuo); left the League (1933); war with <b>China</b> from 1937 (Shanghai, Nanjing); 1941–42 rapid conquests – Pearl Harbor, Philippines, Malaya, Singapore, Dutch East Indies, Gilbert Islands (Tarawa).\nHindrances: US oil embargo (1941); defeat at <b>Midway</b> (1942) lost its carriers; island-hopping (Tarawa 1943, Saipan, Iwo Jima, Okinawa); US submarines cut supplies; firebombing of cities; atomic bombs and Soviet invasion of Manchuria (Aug 1945) → surrender.'}
]}
]},
{y:2022, src:'P2022/KSSC History 2022.pdf', note:'Model answers based on the MOE marking scheme. Question 1 is compulsory, then answer any TWO of Questions 2–6. Essays are given as plans.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Search for security 1945–1990 (compulsory)', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'"The Windmill" cartoon, 1948',a:'1. US Secretary of State <b>George Marshall</b>.\n2. <b>European Recovery Program</b>.\n3. Marshall (the "angel") is bringing American dollars to turn the windmill of Europe\'s economy, which had stopped after WWII.\n4. <b>Marshall Plan (1947)</b>: about $13 billion of US aid to rebuild Western Europe\'s economies so that poverty would not lead people to support communism (containment), and to create markets for US goods.\n5. Stalin called it "dollar imperialism", <b>refused</b> the aid and forced Eastern European countries to refuse it too; he set up <b>Cominform</b> (1947) and <b>Comecon</b> (1949) and tightened control of Eastern Europe – deepening the division of Europe.'},
{n:'B1–6.',q:'Kennedy and Johnson speeches; Vietnam',a:'1. The <b>Vietnam War</b> – Johnson sent combat troops (Marines landed at Da Nang) and began bombing North Vietnam (Operation Rolling Thunder) after the Gulf of Tonkin incident.\n2. The <b>domino theory</b> and containment: if South Vietnam fell to communism, the rest of South-East Asia would follow; the US had also promised to defend South Vietnam.\n3. <b>Richard Nixon</b>.\n4. Gradually withdrawing US troops while training and equipping the South Vietnamese army (ARVN) to do the fighting, with continued US bombing and aid.\n5. Not effective – the ARVN could not hold out alone; after US troops left (1973), North Vietnam captured Saigon in <b>April 1975</b>.\n6. The defeat showed the limits of US power; Americans became reluctant to send troops abroad ("Vietnam syndrome"); Congress passed the War Powers Act (1973) and the US turned to détente with the USSR and China.'},
{n:'C1–7.',q:'Arms race; MAD; arms control',a:'1. The <b>arms race</b> – competition between the USA and USSR to build more and better nuclear weapons.\n2. The <b>USA</b> (first atomic bomb, 1945).\n3. <b>Mutually Assured Destruction</b>.\n4. Each side had enough weapons to destroy the other even after being attacked first, so neither dared to start a nuclear war – the fear of destruction kept the peace (deterrence).\n5. (i) <b>Partial Test Ban Treaty (1963)</b> – banned nuclear tests in the air, space and under water. (ii) <b>SALT I (1972)</b> – limited ICBMs and ABM systems. (Also NPT 1968; INF Treaty 1987 removed all medium-range missiles.)\n6. Preventing an attack by threatening a terrible response.\n7. <b>SALT II (1979)</b>, Carter and Brezhnev: limits on the total number of launchers and warheads (about 2 400). The US Senate never ratified it after the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan (Dec 1979), so détente ended and relations worsened (the "Second Cold War").'},
{n:'D.',q:'Essay: ideologies and foreign policy; one crisis',a:'Plan:\n<b>USA – capitalism and democracy</b>: free elections, private enterprise, individual freedom. Aim: stop communism spreading (containment – Truman Doctrine, Marshall Plan, NATO 1949) and keep open markets.\n<b>USSR – communism</b>: one-party state, state ownership, equality. Aim: security (a buffer zone of friendly states in Eastern Europe after invasions by Germany) and spreading communism (Cominform, Warsaw Pact 1955).\nCrisis – <b>Berlin Blockade (1948–49)</b>: Stalin blocked land routes to West Berlin to force the West out; the US and Britain used containment through the Berlin Airlift (2.3 million tonnes of supplies) without firing a shot; Stalin lifted the blockade; Germany was split into the FRG and GDR, and NATO was formed.\n(Or Cuba 1962 / Korea 1950.)\nConclusion: the opposing ideologies made the superpowers distrust each other and turned Europe into two armed blocs.'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Causes of World War I', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'Cartoon: the Entente Cordiale',a:'1. The <b>Entente Cordiale</b>, 1904.\n2. <b>Britain</b> (the elderly chaperone – France being the young woman).\n3. Britain and France settled their colonial quarrels: France accepted British control of <b>Egypt</b>, and Britain accepted French influence in <b>Morocco</b>.\n4. It ended Britain\'s "splendid isolation"; Germany felt encircled and tested the friendship in the Moroccan crises (1905, 1911), which only drew Britain and France closer; with the Anglo-Russian Entente (1907) it formed the <b>Triple Entente</b>.\n5. Morocco was strategic, at the entrance to the Mediterranean (Gibraltar), and France wanted it to complete its North African empire; Germany wanted a share of colonies and to break the Entente.'},
{n:'B1–6.',q:'Results of the Balkan Wars',a:'1. <b>1912–1913</b>.\n2. <b>Turkey</b> – the <b>Ottoman Empire</b>.\n3. Turkey lost almost all its European land; the Treaty of Bucharest was not accepted as permanent; Bulgaria was bitter; Serbia gained land and became ambitious to unite all Serbs, bringing it into conflict with Austria-Hungary.\n4. "All were ready for another war before long" (or "Bulgaria nursed grievances against Serbia and Greece").\n5. The members of the Balkan League quarrelled over sharing the land won, especially Macedonia; Bulgaria attacked Serbia and Greece and was defeated.\n6. Austria-Hungary had blocked Serbia from getting Albania (a port on the sea), and it ruled about 6 million Serbs and Croats (including Bosnia), whom Serbia wanted to unite.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: why Germany invaded Belgium; how it caused war',a:'Plan:\nWhy: the <b>Schlieffen Plan</b> – to avoid a long two-front war, Germany planned to knock out France in six weeks by sweeping through flat, neutral Belgium (avoiding French border forts), then turn on Russia, which was expected to mobilise slowly. When Russia mobilised (30 July), Germany declared war on Russia and France and demanded passage through Belgium; Belgium refused, and Germany invaded on 4 August 1914.\nHow it caused war: Britain had guaranteed Belgian neutrality in the <b>Treaty of London (1839)</b>; Germany called it a "scrap of paper"; Britain sent an ultimatum and declared war on 4 August, bringing in the British Empire. A Balkan crisis became a world war. Belgian resistance and the BEF also helped stop the plan, leading to trench warfare.\nConclusion.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Origins of World War II', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'Treaty of Versailles (1919 cartoon)',a:'1. The <b>Treaty of Versailles</b> (1919).\n2. (i) War guilt clause – Germany accepted blame (Article 231). (ii) Reparations of £6 600 million. (Also: army limited to 100 000 men, no air force; Rhineland demilitarised; lost Alsace-Lorraine, Polish Corridor and all colonies.)\n3. Germans were furious, calling it a <b>"diktat"</b> (dictated peace) and a "stab in the back"; there were protests and the government that signed it was hated.\n4. Reparations helped cause the hyperinflation of 1923 and economic hardship; national humiliation helped Hitler and the Nazis win support by promising to tear it up.\n5. Germany felt bitter towards France and Britain and wanted revenge; it was excluded from the League at first and later drew closer to the USSR (Rapallo 1922) and Italy; when Hitler broke the treaty, Britain and France appeased him.'},
{n:'B1–6.',q:'Operation Barbarossa',a:'1. <b>Operation Barbarossa</b> (it began on <b>22 June</b> 1941 – the text\'s "July" is a misprint).\n2. Germany and the USSR had signed the <b>Nazi–Soviet Non-Aggression Pact</b> (1939), and Stalin ignored warnings of an attack.\n3. The Soviets retreated using a <b>"scorched earth"</b> policy, moved factories east of the Urals, defended Moscow and Leningrad strongly, and counter-attacked in winter 1941.\n4. (i) The German advance stopped outside Moscow in the harsh winter. (ii) Germany had to fight a long two-front war that drained its men and supplies (leading to defeat at Stalingrad, 1943).\n5. The <b>Nazi–Soviet Pact</b> (Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact, August 1939).\n6. Hitler wanted to invade Poland without fighting the USSR (avoid a two-front war); Stalin wanted time to rearm and a share of Poland and the Baltic states, since he distrusted Britain and France after Munich.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: Hitler\'s successes 1936–39; how invading Russia ended them',a:'Plan:\nSuccesses: <b>Rhineland</b> remilitarised (March 1936) unopposed; Rome–Berlin Axis and Anti-Comintern Pact (1936); <b>Anschluss</b> with Austria (March 1938); <b>Sudetenland</b> gained at Munich (Sept 1938); rest of <b>Czechoslovakia</b> (March 1939); Nazi–Soviet Pact; invasion of Poland (1 Sept 1939) → war. (Then Blitzkrieg victories 1939–41.)\nEnd: Barbarossa (June 1941) opened a huge eastern front; the Russian winter, distance and scorched earth slowed Germany; defeats at Moscow (1941), <b>Stalingrad</b> (1943) and Kursk; the Red Army pushed west to Berlin (1945) while the Allies landed in the West – Germany surrendered in May 1945.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Gandhi and India', qs:[
{n:'A1–6.',q:'John Bull cartoon',a:'1. <b>Britain</b>.\n2. <b>India</b>.\n3. John Bull thinks India is not yet ready to rule itself and must stay under British control ("not yet").\n4. E.g. India\'s huge contribution in <b>World War I</b> (over a million soldiers), or the Amritsar massacre (1919) – Indians demanded self-government (Home Rule).\n5. India was the "<b>jewel in the crown</b>" of the British Empire – a source of raw materials, a market for British goods, soldiers and prestige.\n6. Britain would only give power gradually, when it decided India was ready; it did not trust Indians to govern themselves yet.'},
{n:'B1–5.',q:'Gandhi in "Young India"',a:'1. <b>Swaraj</b> = self-rule/independence – for Gandhi, also freedom from every kind of oppression and self-control of each person.\n2. Untouchability treated millions of Hindus as unclean outcastes, denied them temples, wells and schools – it was injustice and divided Indians; Gandhi called them Harijans ("children of God").\n3. (i) Boycott of British goods/cloth (spinning khadi). (ii) Boycott of British schools, courts and titles; refusing to pay taxes (Salt March).\n4. He wanted freedom for everyone, including untouchables, and unity of Hindus and Muslims built on love, not hatred.\n5. Peaceful refusal to cooperate with British rule – giving up British titles, jobs, schools, courts and goods – so the government could not function, while never using violence.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: three tactics; three events',a:'Tactics: <b>satyagraha</b> (non-violent resistance – Champaran 1917, Kheda 1918); <b>non-cooperation</b> (1920–22, boycott and khadi); <b>civil disobedience</b> (Salt March 1930); fasting and hartals.\nEvents: the <b>Salt March</b> (1930) – world attention, led to the Gandhi–Irwin Pact and Round Table Conference; the <b>Quit India</b> movement (1942) – showed Britain it could not rule India after WWII; his <b>fasts</b> (1947–48) stopped communal violence. He united millions across castes and religions.\nConclusion: with Nehru, Jinnah and WWII also important, Gandhi\'s mass non-violent movement made independence (1947) possible.'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – USA 1920–1945', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'Cartoon: man at desk and "Keep out of war" posters',a:'1. President <b>Franklin D. Roosevelt</b>.\n⚠ The MOE scheme names Theodore Roosevelt; he died in 1919, so he cannot be the president at the start of WWII.\n2. FDR believed the USA should <b>help Britain and France</b> against Hitler because Nazi aggression threatened America too, though he officially kept the USA neutral at first.\n3. (i) WWI had been costly and many felt it was unnecessary – they wanted no more European wars (isolationism). (ii) The USA was still recovering from the Depression and should solve problems at home (Neutrality Acts).\n4. Japan\'s attack on <b>Pearl Harbor</b> (7 December 1941); the USA declared war on Japan, and Germany declared war on the USA.\n5. Neutrality kept Americans out of a costly war; but helping Britain with Cash and Carry (1939) and Lend-Lease (1941) protected US security and trade, since a Nazi-controlled Europe would threaten America.'},
{n:'B1–6.',q:'1920s lifestyle',a:'1. A wealthy, modern consumer lifestyle – cars, radios, electrical goods, entertainment (the "Roaring Twenties").\n2. (i) More comfort and leisure. (ii) Better jobs and higher wages; easier travel by car.\n3. <b>Mass production</b> (assembly line).\n4. <b>Laissez-faire</b>: low taxes, little regulation of business, and high tariffs (Fordney–McCumber 1922) to protect US industry.\n5. New technology and mass production made goods cheaper; electricity spread; the car industry boomed; wages rose; easy credit.\n6. Consumer credit (hire purchase) let people buy goods now and pay later in instalments, so more people could afford cars and appliances and demand increased.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: the New Deal',a:'Plan:\nThe <b>New Deal</b> (1933–): FDR\'s programme of the "3 Rs" – <b>Relief</b>, <b>Recovery</b>, <b>Reform</b>.\nRelief: Emergency Banking Act (bank holiday); FERA gave money to the states for the poor; CCC gave work to young men.\nRecovery: PWA and WPA built roads, dams and schools; TVA developed the Tennessee Valley; AAA raised farm prices; NRA set fair wages and prices.\nReform: Social Security Act (1935) – pensions and unemployment insurance; Wagner Act – union rights; SEC regulated the stock market; FDIC insured bank deposits.\nResults: unemployment fell from 25% to about 14%, confidence returned ("fireside chats"); but full recovery only came with WWII spending.'}
]},
{t:'Q6 – Japan 1912–1945', qs:[
{n:'A1–5.',q:'Pearl Harbor photograph',a:'1. To build an empire – the "<b>Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</b>" – controlling the resources (oil, rubber) of East Asia and the Pacific, free of Western powers.\n2. Japanese carrier aircraft made a <b>surprise air attack</b> on the US Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941.\n3. The US victory at <b>Midway</b> (June 1942) destroyed Japan\'s carriers; then US island-hopping, the submarine blockade, firebombing and finally the <b>atomic bombs</b> on Hiroshima and Nagasaki (Aug 1945).\n4. Japan surrendered (Aug 1945), lost its empire, was occupied by the USA under MacArthur, its army was disbanded and a new democratic constitution (1947) made the Emperor only a symbol.\n5. (i) Ships were moored close together in harbour, not ready for battle. (ii) Burning, sinking ships and few aircraft in the air / sailors with no defence – no warning.'},
{n:'B1–7.',q:'Military agreement of the Axis',a:'1. The <b>Tripartite Pact</b> (Axis military agreement; the pact was 1940, with the military agreement in January 1942).\n2. To divide areas of operation and help each other against their common enemies.\n3. The <b>USA</b> (and Britain).\n4. Japan would mainly operate east of a set line in the Indian Ocean, but could go further if all three agreed later.\n5. Japan would attack them / fight together with Germany and Italy against them.\n6. The US Pacific Fleet was based at Pearl Harbor to face Japan, and Britain\'s bases were in Asia and the Pacific.\n7. They shared the same enemies; Japan\'s war in Asia kept US and British forces busy and away from Europe.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Essay: Japan in the Pacific war 1941; impact by 1945',a:'Plan:\n1941: oil embargo by the USA, Britain and the Netherlands; General Tojo became PM; the attack on <b>Pearl Harbor</b> (7 Dec); rapid invasions of Hong Kong, Malaya, the Philippines, and the Gilbert Islands (Tarawa) in Dec 1941; Singapore fell in Feb 1942.\nImpact by 1945: defeats at Midway, Guadalcanal, <b>Tarawa</b> (1943), Saipan, Iwo Jima and Okinawa; cities destroyed by firebombing; the atomic bombs and Soviet entry (Aug 1945); Emperor Hirohito broadcast the surrender (15 Aug); Tojo\'s military government fell and leaders were tried for war crimes; US occupation and a new democratic constitution.'}
]}
]}
]});
